<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- Evidence badge palette, rendered on every Features row. The colors are part of the honesty
         design: Measured is the only green, and both "no evidence" tiers are red.

         Kept in its own dictionary rather than inline in App.xaml so tests can merge it without
         instantiating the Application class - WPF permits only one Application per AppDomain, and
         loading App.xaml as a dictionary constructs one. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="EvidenceMeasuredBrush" Color="#FF3FB950" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="EvidenceSituationalBrush" Color="#FFD29922" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="EvidenceABBrush" Color="#FF58A6FF" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="EvidenceCosmeticBrush" Color="#FF8B949E" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="EvidenceNoEvidenceBrush" Color="#FFF85149" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="EvidenceNotRecommendedBrush" Color="#FFF85149" />

    <!-- Selected-nav accent. Fixed logo blue rather than the system accent, which on some machines
         is red and makes the selected item read as an error state. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="QuiesceAccentBrush" Color="#FF3FA9FF" />

    <!-- Machine-state palette. ONE KEY PER FACT, and that is the whole reason this block exists:
         #33D29922 previously carried three different meanings across five files. It was the Engaged
         card, it was the Unknown card, and it was the reboot banner - so "your machine is in the
         state you asked for", "Quiesce cannot tell what state your machine is in", and "a change is
         waiting on a restart" all rendered identically. DashboardPage's own comment said "not dirty"
         and "cannot tell" must never render the same, immediately above the branch where they did.

         ENGAGED IS DELIBERATELY NOT AMBER. It is the healthy state this product exists to produce,
         and amber read as a warning about it. It is now the accent blue.

         UNKNOWN IS RED because Quiesce cannot answer the only question it is for, and Engage is
         refused in that state - refusal is a problem, not a caution.

         ORANGE IS DRIFT AND ONLY DRIFT. Nothing else may borrow it, or the one signal added in this
         batch stops meaning anything. AMBER stays for warnings that are not drift: the reboot banner
         and the preflight's save-your-work banner both keep it.

         Alpha 0x26/0x33 because these are card and banner BACKGROUNDS behind body text; the
         Evidence* keys above are 0xFF because they paint glyphs and text. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StateCleanBrush" Color="#263FB950" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StateEngagedBrush" Color="#3358A6FF" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StateDriftBrush" Color="#33F0883E" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StateProblemBrush" Color="#33F85149" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StateWarningBrush" Color="#33D29922" />

    <!-- The neutral result banner. Named because ShowResult's null-brush default was constructing it
         inline on every call, which meant the one background in the app with no name was also the one
         allocated most often. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StateNeutralBrush" Color="#18FFFFFF" />

</ResourceDictionary>
